    "expert_quote": "A benign pigmented band, called longitudinal melanonychia, is usually a single narrow streak, uniform in color and width from cuticle to nail tip, with sharp parallel edges, and it stays essentially unchanged over months... What worries me clinically follows the same ABCDEF framework we use for skin melanoma, adapted for nails. A is age, since new pigmented bands appearing after age fifty deserve more scrutiny. B is a band wider than three millimeters or one with blurred, irregular borders and variegated color rather than uniform brown. C is rapid change in size, shape, or darkness. D is a single digit involved, especially the thumb, big toe, or index finger. E is extension of pigment onto the surrounding skin fold, called Hutchinson's sign, which is a genuine red flag. F is a family or personal history of melanoma. Any band that is new, changing, wider than a pencil eraser's rim, or bleeding through the nail plate should get a same-week dermatology visit and often a nail matrix biopsy rather than a watch-and-wait approach."
